{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user invoked a cobbler orchestrator stitch task (task #5512) for the go-unix-utils project's generation run 44. This is an automated code generation pipeline where the system provides inline project documentation, SRDs, existing source code, and a set of requirements to implement. The task involves implementing Go utilities based on Software Requirements Documents (SRDs), following strict execution constitution rules (no git commands, specification-first development, traceability, etc.). The specific requirements to implement for task #5512 have not yet been fully identified - I was in the process of reading the requirements.yaml file to find which requirements are marked \"in_progress\" for issue 5512.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Go reimplementations of GNU coreutils/moreutils utilities\n   - Differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries (g-prefixed: gcat, gls, etc.)\n   - cobbler-scaffold pipeline: measure (task planning) → stitch (code generation)\n   - pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests pattern for comparing Go vs reference binary output\n   - SIGPIPE handling via sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()\n   - Shared pkg/ libraries: testutils, sys, format, hashutil, sizeparse, encutil\n   - Execution constitution with strict rules: no git, no scope creep, 40-line function limit, 500-line file limit, DRY enforcement\n   - SPDX copyright headers required on all Go files\n   - LC_ALL=C for all differential tests\n   - exec.LookPath + t.Skip pattern for missing reference binaries\n   - Generation branch: task/generation-run44-5512\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `.cobbler/requirements.yaml` (partially read, first 200 lines)\n      - This file is critical for determining what work needs to b