{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XwS8dBaqhXEAchfzmHtW1s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now for the SIGPIPE test — the ref (gbase32) also fails because it exits with \"signal: broken pipe\" rather than exit 0. This means gbase32 actually does NOT exit 0 on SIGPIPE in this particular scenario — it dies from the signal. Go reports signal deaths as errors. Let me check what the b2sum test expects and whether we should skip the ref test.\n\nLooking at the b2sum SIGPIPE test again (line 310): `if refBin, err := exec.LookPath(\"gb2sum\"); err == nil { t.Run(\"ref\", ...) }`. It runs the ref test too, and it passes for b2sum because gb2sum's output is small enough that it all gets written before the pipe closes.\n\nFor base32, the gbase32 ref also fails because gbase32 terminates with SIGPIPE signal (which is normal C behavior — the process gets killed by the signal). The Go test reports this as a non-zero exit. This is actually expected C behavior; the test expectation is wrong for this scenario.\n\nLet me fix the SIGPIPE test to only test the Go binary (not the ref) and accept the fact that C binaries die from SIGPIPE signal:\n\nActually, looking more carefully at gbase32's behavior: it terminates with \"signal: broken pipe\" — this is the default behavior for C programs that don't explicitly handle SIGPIPE. When the process is terminated by SIGPIPE, it's technically not a \"failure\" — it's normal behavior. The shell sees it as exit 0 (128+13 = 141 but shells typically report 0 for head usage).\n\nThe real question is: does our Go binary handle SIGPIPE correctly? Let me rebuild and test."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":345,"cache_read_input_tokens":59873,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":345},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"